      "writtenOn": "2016-10-13T04:40:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The other ones put the ID first.\n\nThe order matters _a lot_ because issued_before is always a range search.\n\nWith the ID first, and criteria like \"audit_id\u003d\u0027foo\u0027 and issued_before \u003e\u003d \u00270000-00-00 00:00:00\u0027\", the index read will be a range query, but it can rapidly jump to the area where audit_id\u003dfoo first, and then continue walking down the b-tree to wherever the range ends. If the ID is second, then the index read has to find _all_ of the events that are \u003e\u003d \u00270000-00-00\u0027 first, and then walk them further to find audit_id.\n\nSo, you should get quite a bit faster results with the ID first.\n\nreference: http://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/5.7/en/range-optimization.html",
      "revId": "a96f997ee7f28cae2ae7a677dca93ec9ca8b4da9",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    }
